    Local Authoring: Core documentation and knowledge bases are maintained locally using self-hosted platforms like Wiki.js and version-controlled via Gitea[cite: 1].

Stage 2: Sanitization & Processing

    Before any documentation or system report is committed to the public mirror, scripts strip out sensitive data.

    Redaction Rules: Public IP addresses, plaintext passwords, API keys, private keys, and specific personal client hostnames are strictly filtered out to prevent exposure.

Stage 3: Public Mirroring (GitOps)

    Sanitized markdown documentation and scripts are synchronized automatically from the local environment to the public GitHub repository (https://github.com/gleason9113/homelab-ops)[cite: 1].

3. Automation Scripts & Execution
System Reporting Script (sys_report.sh)

    Purpose: Automatically extracts host metadata, hardware topology, storage utilization, and active listening services into a standardized Markdown report[cite: 1].

    Execution Requirement: Must be run with root (sudo) privileges to enable ss to resolve active process names and PIDs instead of falling back to "Unknown".

    Usage:
    Bash

    sudo ./scripts/sys_report.sh

4. Security & Compliance Checklist

When updating or adding new documentation to the pipeline, verify the following:

    [ ] No .env or configuration files containing plaintext secrets are staged.

    [ ] Public WAN gateways or external DDNS entry points are generalized or omitted.

    [ ] Personal device names or non-generic client hostnames are scrubbed from topology outputs.


